solnix: tzmon -- survive the ThinkPad false-128C thermal reading on the X1E.

SYMPTOM (ThinkPad X1 Extreme Gen5, 21DE001EUS): the live image boots to a
multi-user login, runs a while, then at a thermal poll the console prints and
the machine halts (photographed 2026-10-18 18:50:51):

  tzmon: WARNING: tzmon: Thermal zone (\_TZ_.THM0) is critically hot (128 C);
  initiating rapid shutdown
  Press any key to reboot.

tzmon's _CRT handler calls mdboot(A_REBOOT, AD_HALT) when _TMP >= _CRT.

WHY 128 C IS NOT REAL, confirmed on the hardware. lixi's \_TZ.THM0 has a SINGLE
trip point, critical = 128000, no _HOT and no _PSV (read live from Pop!_OS:
/sys/class/thermal/thermal_zone0). Its thinkpad_acpi sensors read
`48 46 44 0 46 44 43 -128` -- the machine is 44-48 C, and the 8th sensor is
pegged at -128 == 0x80, the EC "sensor not populated" sentinel. The DSDT's
THM0._TMP returns either a real temperature via _C2K() or EXACTLY TCRT (== _CRT
== 128 C) via an EC-level path that trips on the 0x80 sentinel; there is no
gradient that reaches 128. So on this zone 128 is ALWAYS the sentinel, never
heat. illumos never ran the ThinkPad EC/HKEY init Linux does, so it reads the
raw sentinel and tzmon shuts the machine down.

TWO-PART FIX, both inside tzmon so they run before the first poll (no boot-order
race), gated so non-ThinkPads are unaffected:

1. ThinkPad init (the platform shim): tzmon_attach finds the HKEY device
   (_HID LEN0268/LEN0068/IBM0068), installs a notify handler that drains MHKP
   and logs the real thermal-alarm events (0x6011/0x6012/0x6021/0x6022) with the
   EC's TSL0-TSL3 bitmaps, then calls MHKC(1) -- the init Linux does. Detach and
   the attach-failure paths undo it. No HKEY device -> nothing changes.

2. Sentinel guard (platform-general safety): tzmon_eval_zone refuses to shut
   down on a _TMP that reads back EXACTLY _CRT. A working sensor reports a value
   strictly below its own critical trip until genuine failure; a reading pegged
   exactly at _CRT is the firmware's "unreadable" sentinel. A true critical
   overheat is the EC's hardware cutoff (below the OS), which is unchanged. It
   logs once per zone (rate-limited: the sentinel repeats every poll) and keeps
   polling.

The maintainer's design goal is per-platform init discovered at boot. illumos's
standard mechanism is driver binding by ACPI _HID; part 1 is the first such
shim (it binds on the ThinkPad HKEY _HID). Part 2 is the model-independent floor
that keeps a bad sensor from powering a machine off.

